export const FR_C1_S07_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "fr-c1-l-erreur-de-mechain": [
    "In the spring of 1805, Jean-Baptiste Delambre received at the Paris Observatory three iron-bound wooden crates, sent from Spain. They contained the papers of Pierre Méchain, his colleague and rival, who had died a few months earlier of yellow fever in a village on the Valencian coast. Delambre opened them with an emotion he would have admitted to no one. For seven years the two men had surveyed France, one in the north, the other in the south, to measure the meridian that links Dunkirk to Barcelona.",
    "The undertaking had been born of the Revolution's wildest ambition: to give the world a universal unit of measurement, drawn from nature itself and not from the foot of a king. The metre would be one ten-millionth of the distance between the North Pole and the equator. Still, that distance had to be known. It was to calculate it that the two astronomers had set out in 1792 with their repeating circles, their telescopes and their passports signed by a Republic that half of Europe wanted to bring down.",
    "In the north, Delambre had experienced arrests, demolished church towers that he had used as sighting points, peasants who took his signals for messages to the enemy. But Méchain had experienced worse. Stuck in Barcelona by the war between France and Spain, he had stayed for months unable to return, injured in an accident with a water pump that had almost cost him his life. And he had come back to Paris in 1798 transformed: taciturn, touchy, stubbornly refusing to hand over his original notebooks.",
    "It took Delambre several weeks to understand why. In the Barcelona notebooks, crossed out and written over, he found two series of observations of the city's latitude, taken a few months apart from two different places: the fortress of Montjuïc and an inn in the centre, the Fontana de Oro. They should have agreed. They did not. The discrepancy was three seconds of arc, about a hundred metres on the ground. For an astronomer of Méchain's calibre, it was an abyss.",
    "Méchain had seen the error as early as 1794. He had been unable to go back and check his measurements, since the war prevented him. He had told no one. He had published the value that seemed most reliable to him and hidden the other. For ten years he had lived with this secret like a shameful disease, convinced that he had compromised the metre itself, and with it the honour of French science. It was to atone for this fault that he had gone back to Spain in 1803, past sixty, to extend the measurement to the Balearic Islands. There he had met his death.",
    "Delambre sat for a long time in front of those pages. He knew now what Méchain had not known: the discrepancy did not come from any mistake by his colleague. The instruments, the refraction of the air, the very irregularities of the Earth, which is not the perfect sphere people had imagined, were enough to explain it. Méchain had not made an error; he had measured, with terrible accuracy, the imperfection of the world. And he had died of shame over it.",
    "It remained to decide what to do with this discovery. Delambre could publish everything and tarnish forever the memory of a man who could no longer defend himself. He could also keep it all quiet. He chose a middle way, which was typical of him. In the great work in which he presented the expedition's results, he discreetly corrected the figures, without accusing anyone. Then he wrote a detailed note on the affair, which he deposited in the Observatory's archives, for those who would come after him.",
    "The standard metre, cast in platinum, had already been deposited in the National Archives in 1799. It was, as we know today, too short by about two tenths of a millimetre. No one thought of changing it. It has remained the measure of the world, with its tiny flaw, like a signature. It is perhaps not absurd that the most rational unit ever invented should carry within it, invisible, the trace of a man who never forgave himself for being fallible.",
  ],
  "fr-c1-paris-sous-les-eaux": [
    "In January 1910 the Seine burst its banks, and Paris discovered that the river was not, as people believed, tamed. It had rained without respite for weeks over the Paris basin; the tributaries had swollen, the waterlogged soil could absorb nothing more. On the 21st, the river began to rise by a metre a day. Parisians got into the habit of going to the Pont de l'Alma to consult the statue of the Zouave, the stone soldier whose legs, then belly, then chest disappeared beneath the waves. By the 28th the water was up to his shoulders.",
    "Émile Garnier was twenty-two and worked in the sewers. He was among the first to understand that the Seine was not content to overflow along the embankments: it was rising from below, through the pipes, the cellars, the tunnels of the brand-new metro. Whole neighbourhoods, far from the river, found themselves flooded without having seen a single wave coming. In the Rue de Lille and the Rue de Bourgogne, water welled up from the drains like an evil fountain.",
    "He was given a rowing boat and a mission: to evacuate the residents of the seventh arrondissement who wished to leave, and to bring supplies to the others. He rowed from morning to night between the Haussmann façades, beneath windows from which bourgeois in dressing gowns lowered baskets to him on the end of a rope. Everywhere, walkways of planks had been laid on trestles, where people moved about in precarious balance, umbrella in one hand, hat in the other.",
    "On the third day, a maid called to him from a balcony in the Rue de Verneuil. Madame did not want to leave the apartment, but Madame needed him. Émile recognised the address with a pang. This was where Mme Delorme-Vasseur lived, the owner of the building in the Rue Mouffetard where he rented a room under the roof, and who had let him know the week before, through her notary, that she would throw him out if he was even one more day late with his rent.",
    "He climbed the service stairs, soaked to the knees, and found the landlady in the drawing room, very upright, surrounded by trunks she had not had the heart to pack. She did not recognise him. No doubt she had never seen her tenants. What she wanted to save was neither her jewels nor her paintings, but a gilded cage in which an African grey parrot, grey with a red tail, was fidgeting and repeating in a hoarse voice: \"What times! What times!\"",
    "His name was Bonaparte, he had belonged to Madame's late husband, and he could not bear the damp. The veterinarian lived on the other side of the Seine, on the Right Bank, which had so far been spared. Émile agreed. He crossed the Esplanade des Invalides by boat, the cage on his knees, under the astonished gaze of onlookers, while the parrot greeted each walkway with a resounding \"What times!\" that made even the most exhausted rescuers laugh.",
    "In the evening, when he brought Bonaparte back restored to health, wrapped in a wool blanket, Mme Delorme-Vasseur wanted to give him a gold coin. Émile refused it. He simply asked that she kindly consider that the tenant in the Rue Mouffetard, the one on the sixth floor overlooking the courtyard, might be a few days late for a good reason. She looked at him for a long time, understood, and blushed, which, according to the maid, had not happened to her since the World's Fair.",
    "The waters began to go down the next day. It took weeks to empty the cellars, months to dry the walls, years to forget the smell. Émile never received another letter from the notary. But in the spring a parcel arrived at the Rue Mouffetard, with no accompanying note: a rent receipt marked paid for the whole of 1910. Bonaparte, it is said, lived another twenty years. He had added a new word to his repertoire, which he shouted every time it rained: \"Zouave!\"",
  ],
  "fr-c1-les-quatre-crochets": [
    "For twenty-eight years I guarded the Salon Carré of the Louvre. People often ask me which painting I liked best there. They expect me to answer the Mona Lisa. The truth is that I only really looked at her once, on the day she was no longer there. I am writing these lines in 1938, when there is talk of war again and the masterpieces, they say, are already being packed into crates. It seems useful to tell what I saw, before my memory, too, gets put in a crate.",
    "On Monday August 21, 1911, the museum was closed to the public. It was the day for cleaning, repairs and photographers. The corridors were swarming with workmen in white smocks, whom nobody thought of questioning. At around seven I passed one on the service stairs, a small dark man with a moustache, carrying under his arm something wrapped in a sheet. He greeted me politely. I returned his greeting. I even helped him, I think, to find the door leading to the Cour Visconti, because he seemed lost.",
    "It was not until the Tuesday that the absence was noticed. A painter who had come to copy a nearby canvas was surprised to see nothing on the wall but four iron hooks. He was told that the painting was no doubt with the photographers. People waited. The photographers did not have it. Around midday they had to face the facts: the Mona Lisa had disappeared. The museum was closed for a whole week, the guards questioned one after another. I told them about the workman on the stairs. Nobody knew what to do with my statement.",
    "The press had a field day. Suspicion fell on the Germans, American bankers, anarchists, a billionaire collector. The poet Guillaume Apollinaire was arrested, guilty of having associated with an adventurer who had once stolen statuettes from the museum; his friend was questioned, a young Spanish painter called Picasso, who swore he had never borrowed anything. They were released, but the shame stayed with them for a long time. As for the prefect of police, every morning he promised an imminent arrest that never came.",
    "But the strangest thing happened when the museum reopened. Parisians crowded into the Salon Carré, not to see the Titians or the Veroneses, but to contemplate the empty space. They stood there for whole minutes in front of that rectangle of wall a little paler than the rest and its four hooks. Some laid flowers, as on a grave. I had never seen as many people in front of the Mona Lisa as in front of her absence. Until then she had been just one fine painting among others. The void made her a legend.",
    "She was found two years later, in December 1913, in Florence. The thief was called Vincenzo Peruggia, an Italian glazier who had worked at the Louvre, fitting, as it happens, the protective glass on the paintings. He claimed he had wanted to give back to Italy a masterpiece that Napoleon had supposedly stolen from it, which was untrue, since Leonardo had brought it to France himself. He had kept the canvas for two years in the false bottom of a trunk, in his room on the Rue de l'Hôpital-Saint-Louis, a stone's throw from where I lived.",
    "When I saw his photograph in the newspaper, I recognised the moustache, the polite manner, the slightly lost look. It was my workman from the stairs. I said nothing about it, except to my wife. What would have been the point? You don't convict a guard for being courteous. But for a long time I carried this small weight: of having held the door, perhaps, for the thief of the century, and wished him a good day.",
    "The Mona Lisa returned to the Louvre on January 4, 1914, to cheers. She was hung in the same place, on the same hooks. Then I did what I had never done: I stopped in front of her and looked at her for a long time. She was smiling, as always, with that air of knowing something we don't. I think I know, today, what it was. She knew that to become the most famous painting in the world, all she had needed to do was disappear. And that I had a little something to do with it.",
  ],
  "fr-c1-le-compteur-tournait": [
    "On September 6, 1914, at around five in the evening, Marcel Lefèvre was waiting for a fare at the taxi rank on the Place de Clichy when a policeman came up to his car, a red and black Renault belonging to the taxi company. The policeman did not want to go to the Gare de l'Est. He handed him a requisition order, signed in the name of General Gallieni, military governor of Paris. All the taxis in the capital were to gather on the Esplanade des Invalides, with full tanks, for an unknown destination.",
    "The Germans were less than fifty kilometres away. Some evenings you could hear the guns from Montmartre. The government had fled to Bordeaux, and the Parisians who could afford it had followed. Joffre's army was trying to hold on the Marne, and men were lacking where they were needed. The trains were not enough. Someone at headquarters had had an idea that everyone found absurd, then brilliant: to send the infantry to the front by taxi.",
    "Marcel drove all night, headlights off so as not to be spotted, in a column of several hundred cars moving at walking pace, bumper to bumper, along the road to Nanteuil-le-Haudouin. He was carrying five soldiers of the 104th Infantry Regiment, crammed onto the back seat and the folding seat, rifles between their knees. They hardly spoke. One slept, another smoked non-stop. The meter, which no one had thought of stopping, ticked away in the dark with its regular little clicking.",
    "The youngest sat next to him, in the front. His name was Yves Le Goff, he came from Plougastel, near Brest, and he had never been in a motor car. He looked at the dashboard with a child's curiosity, asking what each lever was for. Around three in the morning, while the column had stopped on the verge, he took a crumpled envelope from his greatcoat and handed it to Marcel. \"It's for my mother. If I don't come back, will you post it? I don't trust the army postman.\"",
    "Marcel took the letter, slipped it into the inside pocket of his jacket and promised. At daybreak he dropped his five passengers at the entrance to a village whose name he never remembered, a few kilometres from the fighting. Yves shook his hand very hard, the way people do in the country. Then he ran off to join the others, his rifle, too big for him, banging against his legs. Marcel turned round, with a heavy heart and a meter showing an astronomical sum.",
    "Against all expectations, the army paid the fare. The accounts were settled at the meter rate, minus a discount, and each driver received what he was owed a few weeks later, which caused much laughter in the trenches and much grumbling in Parliament. There has been a great deal of debate since about the real importance of these taxis in the victory of the Marne. Historians say they carried only a few thousand men, a drop in the ocean in a battle that involved a million. Marcel never doubted that they had counted.",
    "He kept the letter for four years. He never opened it, never posted it, since nothing proved that its writer was dead. It stayed in the drawer of his bedside table, under his military record book, and his wife learned not to touch it. Every time the lists of the missing were published in the newspaper, he looked for the name Le Goff. He found it three times. It was never the right first name.",
    "In the spring of 1919, a thin man with a hollow face, one jacket sleeve empty and pinned to his chest, turned up at the taxi rank on the Place de Clichy and asked for the driver Lefèvre. Marcel recognised him by the way he looked at the dashboard. Yves had come to collect his letter. He put it in his pocket without opening it, he too. Then he asked, a little embarrassed, how much he owed for the 1914 fare. Marcel burst out laughing, and they went for a drink at the bar across the road. This time, the meter did not run.",
  ],
  "fr-c1-les-bras-du-telegraphe": [
    "Before the electric wire, France spoke across the rooftops. Since the Revolution, towers topped with long jointed arms had covered the country, planted on the hills every ten or fifteen kilometres. This was the telegraph invented by Claude Chappe. In each tower, a \"stationnaire\" watched the previous tower through a telescope, reproduced the position of its arms with cranks, and the next tower did the same. A message left Paris and reached Bordeaux in a few hours, when stagecoaches took almost four days.",
    "The operators did not understand what they were transmitting. The signals were coded; only the directors, at the two ends of the line, had the vocabulary. They were required to have good eyes, patience and iron discipline. They were badly paid. Pierre Garnaud was nineteen when he was appointed in 1836 to the tower at Tours, replacing an old operator, Guibout, who had fallen seriously ill. He liked the job: he felt he was holding the kingdom's nervous system in his hands.",
    "One evening in August, Guibout's wife came to fetch him. The old man, who knew he was dying, wanted to speak to him. Pierre found him in bed, his complexion grey, his voice barely audible. \"You must know, lad. I don't want to take this with me.\" And Guibout, in broken sentences, told him a story that Pierre took several minutes to believe.",
    "Two years earlier, a man had come to see him, an emissary of two bankers from Bordeaux, the Blanc brothers. He had offered him money, a great deal of money for an operator, in exchange for a tiny service. From time to time Guibout would receive a parcel of gloves by post. If the gloves were white, he was to slip into the official message he was relaying a specific stray signal, followed at once by the error signal. If the gloves were grey, another one. The error would be corrected at the next tower. No one would notice a thing.",
    "No one, except an accomplice posted near Bordeaux, who watched the line with a telescope. The false signal, although erased immediately, travelled from tower to tower all the way to him. It said, in short, only one thing: government bonds have gone up on the Paris stock exchange, or they have gone down. The Blanc brothers thus knew the prices several days before their Bordeaux competitors, who waited for the stagecoach. They had made a fortune. And the message arrived by way of gloves, because the post was too slow to carry a piece of news, but fast enough to carry a code.",
    "Pierre didn't sleep a wink that night. The next day, after much hesitation, he went to see the director of the line. The investigation was quick; Guibout died before he learned its outcome. The Blanc brothers were arrested, along with the operators they had corrupted, and tried at Tours in 1837. Pierre gave evidence, in a borrowed suit, before a packed courtroom where journalists had been brought in from Paris.",
    "The verdict astounded everyone. The bankers were acquitted. No law, the court observed, forbade using the state's telegraph for private purposes, for the simple reason that nobody had imagined it could be done. Only the bribing of officials could have been punished, but the evidence was thin. The Blanc brothers walked out free and rich, and went off, it is said, to found casinos in Germany.",
    "A few months later, Parliament hastily passed a law reserving to the State the monopoly on long-distance transmissions. Pierre remained an operator until the electric telegraph made the towers useless, twenty years later. He liked to tell people that he had witnessed the first swindle in the history of telecommunications, and the first law made too late to punish it. He would add, thoughtfully, that it would no doubt not be the last.",
  ],
};

